A struct, a list and a map are values, and a store copies one
(passValueCopy, ir/valuecopy.go). Each language writes the copy out from the
value's type: Go clones a slice or map and copies a struct's fields that share
storage, JavaScript spreads an object and maps an array, Kotlin copies a data
class and makes a new mutable list. A struct that can hold itself is a function
calling itself. A literal is nobody else's and is not copied. A parameter
is passed by value: a function that modifies one binds its own copy at entry,
one that replaces it whole binds it uncopied, and one that only reads it
binds nothing.

